  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\n   The proposed approach represents the function h(x) as a superposition of shifted indicator functions and uses a novel optimization strategy based on probabilistic programming and Bayesian optimization. The core idea is to parameterize the base function using a small set of basis functions and then optimize their weights. This differs from previous strategies by:\n   - Using a physics-inspired framework with continuity constraints\n   - Incorporating Bayesian optimization that explicitly accounts for epistemic uncertainty in the evaluation of continuous functions\n   - Leveraging warping and adaptive sampling based on runtime performance metrics\n   \n   The expected improvement over the parent solution comes from:\n   - Better handling of continuous domain by parameterizing functions instead of discrete samples\n   - Reduced curse of dimensionality through functional approximation\n   - More efficient exploration via guided sampling based on certified bounds\n\n   The algorithm's bottlenecks include the expensive evaluation of the compositional optimization landscape, which is mitigated by adaptive budget allocation and schema steering.\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\n   We represent h(x) as a composition of basis functions:\n   h(x) = \u03a3_{i=1}^M a_i * I(x - t_i) * \u03c6(x)\n   \n   Subject to:\n   \u222b_0\u00b2 h(x) dx = 1\n   0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1 for all x\n   \n   The optimization is performed over continuous parameters {a_i, t_i} with the constraint that the composed function satisfies both integral and unit bounds.\n\n   The objective C5 is defined as:\n   C5 = max_k \u222b_0^2 h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx\n   \n   We incorporate Bayesian probability into the evaluation process, formally:\n   P(C5 \u2264 \u03c4 | Data) = \u222b P(C5 \u2264 \u03c4 | Model, Data) P(Model | Data) dModel\n   \n   This provides a probabilistically informed descent mechanism.\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\n   - Parameterize the solution through continuous basis functions\n   - Initialize from parent solution with functional transformation\n   - Set up Gaussian Process regressors for evaluation components\n   - Implement Bayesian Optimization with expected improvement acquisition\n   - Incorporate adaptive budget management with time-level caching\n   - Use runtime-calibrated execution schemas\n   - Maintain probabilistic bounds verification\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations:\n   - Primary schema: scaled triangular waveforms with continuity constraints\n   - Alternative base functions: trigonometric and splines (fallback)\n   - Sample size evolution: geometric from 16 to 160 points\n   - Success indicators: gradient descent metrics, confidence intervals\n   - Budget adaptation: based on observed improvement rate\n\n5. Budget and robustness:\n   - Global allocation: 40% exploration, 60% refinement\n   - Time checkpoints: monitor at 20%, 40%, 60%, 80% mark\n   - Fallback procedures: geometric mean interpolation when parameters degraded\n   - Timeout recovery: revert to parent structure at critical points\n\n6. Validation and return contract:\n   - Evaluate final candidate through double-check correlation computation\n   - Verify integral and bounds to 1e-7 tolerance\n   - Maintain probabilistic bounds to demonstrate confidence\n   - Return h_values and C5 with qualification metrics\n\nNotes implementation challenges include handling basis representation with affine transformations and probabilistic evaluation that may introduce numerical artifacts. These are mitigated by progressive functional approximation and bounded extrapolation.",
